Distributed P2P Battle Simulator
Liam Kandel, Ryan Nageer, Kaitlyn Franklin
COP5570
Project Description
The objective of this project is to develop a Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Distributed War Battle
Simulator designed to run across multiple nodes on the linprog cluster. Unlike traditional
client-server games, this system will utilize a decentralized architecture where players on
different machines (e.g., linprog1, linprog2, linprog3) can instantiate custom armies and engage
in a synchronized, real-time battle simulation.
What will you be doing?
● P2P Network Implementation: Establishing socket-based communication between
separate linprog nodes to share game state without a central server.
● Distributed Simulation Engine: Developing a text-based battle engine that processes
combat logic locally on each node and synchronizes outcomes.
● Concurrency Management: Using multi-threading to handle simultaneous network I/O,
user input, and AI combat cycles.
● AI Combat Logic: Implementing an autonomous system that dictates unit behavior
and engagement rules during the simulation.
Why is this project important/interesting?
Distributed gaming presents unique challenges in state consistency and latency. Building a
battle simulator on a shared cluster like linprog provides a practical environment to explore
how independent systems coordinate in real-time without a centralized controller.
Relationship to COP5570
This project directly applies several core concepts from Advanced Unix Programming:
1. Network Programming: TCP/UDP sockets for communication across hosts.
2. Multi-threading/POSIX Threads: Concurrent game UI and network handling.
3. Synchronization: Mutex/condition variable coordination between gameplay phases.
4. Distributed Systems: Node-to-node connection setup, discovery flow, and observer mode.
Estimated Lines of Code (LOC)
The current implementation is within the expected 1,200 – 1,800 line target range across
networking, battle simulation, protocol, and interface modules.
